import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EvidenceBadge from '../../components/EvidenceBadge.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="How JustDaddit Researches and Scores Gear" description="The evidence labels, scoring rules, source hierarchy, recall checks, corrections, affiliate policy, and hands-on standards behind JustDaddit reviews." canonicalPath="/how-we-research/">
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="page-hero"><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'How we research' }]} /><p class="eyebrow">Editorial standard · Version 1.0</p><h1>How we research</h1><p class="page-lede">The label on a review should tell you what work actually happened. “Best” is not a method. A long article is not automatically a careful one.</p></div></section>
    <div class="article-shell">
      <nav class="jump-nav"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#labels">Evidence labels</a><a href="#sources">Source order</a><a href="#score">Scoring</a><a href="#review">Review process</a><a href="#safety">Safety and recalls</a><a href="#money">Affiliate policy</a><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></nav>
      <article class="article-content">
        <section id="labels"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Say what happened</p><h2>Four evidence labels</h2><div class="comparison-grid"><article class="comparison-point"><EvidenceBadge /><h3>Research Review</h3><p>No JustDaddit hands-on use. Built from primary documentation, independent evaluation, owner evidence, support data, recalls, and dated commerce information.</p></article><article class="comparison-point"><EvidenceBadge type="Owner-Verified" text="Structured owner evidence" /><h3>Owner-Verified</h3><p>A verified owner supplied photos, exact model details, and structured observations. This is not the same as a full editorial test.</p></article><article class="comparison-point"><EvidenceBadge type="Hands-On" text="Documented test protocol" /><h3>Hands-On</h3><p>JustDaddit used the exact model through a published category protocol and retained the notes, conditions, and limitations.</p></article><article class="comparison-point"><EvidenceBadge type="Long-Term" text="Six months or more" /><h3>Long-Term</h3><p>The exact product was used for at least six months, with maintenance, failures, changes, and continuing costs recorded.</p></article></div><p>A page can be updated from one label to another. Earlier research does not disappear. It becomes part of the update record.</p></section>
        <section id="sources"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Evidence order</p><h2>Not all sources answer the same question</h2><ol><li><strong>Exact manufacturer documents:</strong> identity, dimensions, included equipment, compatibility, manuals, warranties, and stated claims.</li><li><strong>Regulatory and safety databases:</strong> recalls, warnings, and product-safety actions.</li><li><strong>Independent measurement or evaluation:</strong> performance under stated conditions and direct comparisons.</li><li><strong>Parts and support records:</strong> diagrams, availability, replacement paths, and service terms.</li><li><strong>Owner evidence:</strong> recurring use, maintenance, annoyances, and questions that controlled reviews can miss.</li><li><strong>Retailer data:</strong> current price, inventory, bundles, package contents, and variant confusion.</li></ol><p>Manufacturer claims are attributed. Owner anecdotes are never converted into failure percentages. Retailer copy does not become editorial analysis.</p></section>
        <section id="score"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Scoring</p><h2>The score supports the verdict. It does not replace it.</h2><p>Product reviews use a five-point overall editorial score supported by six ten-point category scores:</p><div class="rating-list"><div class="rating-row"><span>Family fit</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Context</strong></div><div class="rating-row"><span>Control & learning</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Skill</strong></div><div class="rating-row"><span>Durability & repair</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Life</strong></div><div class="rating-row"><span>Setup & ownership</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Effort</strong></div><div class="rating-row"><span>Performance</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Use</strong></div><div class="rating-row"><span>Value</span><span class="rating-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><strong>Price</strong></div></div><p>The overall score is not a mathematical average. Family fit can outweigh performance. Unsafe or unsupported use can disqualify a product regardless of technical ability. Scores can change when price, product generation, parts support, or evidence changes.</p></section>
        <section id="review"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Research review workflow</p><h2>From exact product to publishable decision</h2><ol><li>Assign a permanent product ID and confirm exact model, generation, region, and variants.</li><li>Collect the manual, specifications, warranty, support documents, parts diagrams, and compatible accessories.</li><li>Check current US retail availability, package contents, price date, and affiliate path.</li><li>Search CPSC and manufacturer safety notices.</li><li>Review credible independent evaluations and document their conditions.</li><li>Map owner-report themes without inventing frequency.</li><li>Calculate the first-weekend cost and likely continuing ownership costs.</li><li>Write best-for, skip-if, alternatives, limitations, and the verdict.</li><li>Validate links, schema, mobile layout, dates, claims, image records, and visible FAQs.</li><li>Schedule material facts for rechecking.</li></ol><div class="callout"><strong>Publication threshold</strong><p>If the exact product identity, active availability, evidence, or affiliate path is too weak, the page stays in research. A keyword is not a reason to publish.</p></div></section>
        <section id="safety"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Safety and recalls</p><h2>Practical warnings, not decorative disclaimers</h2><p>Every relevant review checks the US Consumer Product Safety Commission recall database and manufacturer notices before publication. The result is dated. Absence from a search is not a permanent guarantee.</p><p>Battery-powered ride-ons, fast RC vehicles, trampolines, play systems, tents, and roof cargo all carry category-specific risks. The review explains who controls setup, supervision, charging, installation, inspection, and maintenance.</p><p>JustDaddit does not recommend unapproved battery substitutions, bypassed safety systems, modified load ratings, or clever shortcuts that move risk from the product into the family.</p></section>
        <section id="money"><p class="eyebrow">06 / Affiliate policy</p><h2>Commission may fund the work. It does not buy the verdict.</h2><p>JustDaddit may earn a commission when a reader buys through a qualifying link. The disclosure appears before the first commercial link. Affiliate relationships do not determine the score, inclusion, winner, or criticism.</p><p>Products without affiliate programs can still win. Products with high commissions can lose or be excluded. Reference prices are dated, and a retailer button should never pretend an old price is live.</p></section>
        <section id="corrections"><p class="eyebrow">07 / Corrections</p><h2>When the evidence changes, the page should show it</h2><p>Material corrections include the date, what changed, and why. Quiet typo fixes do not need a public record. Model identity, safety, compatibility, included equipment, pricing logic, scores, and verdict changes do.</p><p>Readers and manufacturers can submit evidence. A request is not a correction by itself. The underlying claim must be checked against stronger or more current documentation.</p><div class="final-verdict"><p class="eyebrow">The standard in one sentence</p><h2>Tell the reader what you know, how you know it, and where the answer could still be wrong.</h2></div></section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
